00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:33.490
that might be of interest to you as well.

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:35.450
And today's video was actually inspired

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:37.078
by many of your comments.

00:01:37.078 --> 00:01:39.490
So whenever I've created
one of these videos

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:41.200
that was about slide writing in the past,

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.097
many of you told me Heinrich,

00:01:43.097 --> 00:01:45.620
aren't these slides far too full?

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:48.200
Why is there so much text on these pages?

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:51.460
Is this really a great way of
designing and layouting slides

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:54.230
if you actually intend to
present them in the end?

00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:56.720
So this is what I want to address today.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:57.780
Let's do that by looking

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:00.620
into different business
types of presentations.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:04.070
And I like to distinguish
three different styles

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:04.930
of presentations.

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:06.500
For sure, you might be able to come up

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.110
with more different ones.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:09.103
But these from my perspective

00:02:09.103 --> 00:02:11.920
are probably the most
important, most relevant ones.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:14.474
And the first style is the
style that I like to call

00:02:14.474 --> 00:02:16.362
the Steve Jobs style.

00:02:16.362 --> 00:02:18.840
These big keynote presentations,

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:22.800
as you often find them for
B2C product introductions,

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.330
where people are standing on a huge stage,

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:27.200
presenting to a large audience,

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:29.870
for instance about a new
product that is being launched

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:31.550
that is being introduced to the market.

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:33.100
So what is the thing
about these presentations?

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:37.010
Well, often there only a couple
of pictures on the slides,

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:39.150
maybe only a couple of words.

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:41.500
It's really for a large stage.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:42.503
And what's really specific here

00:02:42.503 --> 00:02:46.470
is that these presentations
are intended to be presented.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:47.930
They absolutely require

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:50.741
that somebody stands
in front of the screen,

00:02:50.741 --> 00:02:52.750
in front of the big canvas,

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:56.200
and give some explanations,
provide some soundtrack,

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:59.070
to the slides, explain
what is really shown here.

00:02:59.070 --> 00:02:59.903
Or in other words,

00:02:59.903 --> 00:03:01.920
if you would just send
around the slide deck,

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:04.626
without anybody explaining
it, probably most cases

00:03:04.626 --> 00:03:06.963
for most people, it
would be quite difficult

00:03:06.963 --> 00:03:08.910
to really understand all the details

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:10.760
about what this is
supposed to be all about.

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:12.740
So whenever I get comments of people

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:15.270
saying to me that all the
presentations that I use

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:16.740
they have too much text,

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:18.820
they're not clean, not minimal enough.

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:20.220
I'd assume that probably people

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:22.070
have this style in their mind.

00:03:22.070 --> 00:03:24.330
Of course, the Steve Jobs
style has also become

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:26.980
quite well known, quite well studied.

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:28.795
So people assume that
this is also the style

00:03:28.795 --> 00:03:32.630
of presentations that
businesses should be conducted.

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:35.300
But this is not the style
that I'm talking about

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:36.170
on my channel.

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:39.048
And indeed, this is also not
the way how consulting firms

00:03:39.048 --> 00:03:43.080
at least in most cases, prepare
their slide presentations.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:44.370
So let's contrast this

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:46.618
with a second use case of presentations.

00:03:46.618 --> 00:03:49.524
I often like to think about
this as informal meetings.

00:03:49.524 --> 00:03:52.195
So imagine yourself preparing a one-on-one

00:03:52.195 --> 00:03:53.460
with your team lead,

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:55.950
or maybe an informal session
with some of your colleagues,

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:57.277
where you want to do some brainstorming,

00:03:57.277 --> 00:03:59.360
and just bounce back some ideas,

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:02.490
prepare a couple of thoughts
as a discussion basis.

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:03.630
And here, of course,

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:07.000
the objective is just to
quickly create a short document

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.667
without you now needing
lots of time to create it,.

00:04:09.667 --> 00:04:12.245
but enables you to have a
good and fruitful discussion

00:04:12.245 --> 00:04:13.120
with your team.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:14.440
It's an important characteristic

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:16.390
of this style of business presentations

00:04:16.390 --> 00:04:19.350
is that there's no need
to reuse these slides.

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:20.190
These slides are really

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:22.290
just for usually one or two interactions

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:23.490
where you discuss topics.

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:26.050
There's no need to have this really ready

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:28.510
for a larger audience and
additional one comment

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:29.760
that often people say is,

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:32.490
Heinrich, do you really need
to invest that much time

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:35.000
into every single
presentation that you do?

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:37.149
Do you always need to create presentations

00:04:37.149 --> 00:04:40.137
in the style that meet
these really high standards

00:04:40.137 --> 00:04:41.670
of slight writing,

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:43.560
these high standards of presentations,

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:46.390
how large consulting
firms like McKinsey, BCG

00:04:46.390 --> 00:04:47.362
or Bain do it?

00:04:47.362 --> 00:04:49.916
And here my answer would
be well, no, of course not.

00:04:49.916 --> 00:04:53.030
Of course this style is absolutely valid

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:55.360
if you just quickly want to
bounce back a couple of ideas,

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:57.820
just have a meeting and
interaction with your team.

00:04:57.820 --> 00:05:00.493
Don't waste too much
time on perfect layout,

00:05:00.493 --> 00:05:03.250
on perfect communication on the slides.

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:04.715
Keep it short, keep it simple.

00:05:04.715 --> 00:05:06.920
Make adjustments in the
way that enables you

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:09.220
to have this discussion
that you do want to have.

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:10.300
So if this is clear to you,

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:13.190
then you might ask yourself,
then what is this style?

00:05:13.190 --> 00:05:16.105
What is this consulting style
of creating presentations?

00:05:16.105 --> 00:05:18.480
How does this now differ
from the other ones?

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:21.730
And indeed these presentations
are often more formal ones.

00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:24.073
You create them for more formal audiences,

00:05:24.073 --> 00:05:28.540
for senior management, or
board-level members of a company.

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:30.380
These are presentations that you create

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:33.114
to report on the progress of your project.

00:05:33.114 --> 00:05:35.302
And indeed, what's probably
also very interesting

00:05:35.302 --> 00:05:37.840
is that these presentations are often

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:39.410
not even presented.

00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:40.500
So often in these meetings,

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:43.059
they're not even projected
to a wall, to a screen.

00:05:43.059 --> 00:05:46.060
But people just get a
handout of the document

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:46.943
that then they have in front of you.

00:05:46.943 --> 00:05:48.693
And indeed what also frequently happens

00:05:48.693 --> 00:05:51.140
is that at least then after the meeting,

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:53.860
these presentations are being
sent around the organization.

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:56.390
People just forward them to other people.

00:05:56.390 --> 00:05:58.427
And then other read these documents.

00:05:58.427 --> 00:06:00.990
We're not sitting in the original meeting

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:03.500
where maybe the document
was initially presented.

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:05.270
And indeed, I very well
remember an instance,

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:06.790
I was working for a company,

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:09.050
and at this company a couple of years ago,

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:09.986
like two or three years ago,

00:06:09.986 --> 00:06:12.940
BCG, Boston Consulting Group,
made a project with them.

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:15.220
And as a final reside of this project,

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:16.560
there was now a document

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:18.128
that Boston Consulting Group created.

00:06:18.128 --> 00:06:19.367
This was also in the style

00:06:19.367 --> 00:06:21.790
of a big PowerPoint presentation.

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:22.780
And up to this day,

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:25.070
this document is still being referenced

00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:28.590
and sent around, and called
just the BCG Document.

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:30.190
And from this anecdote alone,

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:32.480
there are some really important learnings.

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:35.260
So the first is that
these documents, again,

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:37.750
are not necessarily
intended to be presented.

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:39.970
It's important that people can understand

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:42.370
every message of these documents,

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:44.660
if they just read it for themselves.

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:45.938
So every single slide,

00:06:45.938 --> 00:06:49.012
every single page in such a document,

00:06:49.012 --> 00:06:53.420
needs to be self-explanatory,
needs to speak for itself.

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:56.426
And this of course requires
a higher level of detail,

00:06:56.426 --> 00:07:00.520
often a higher level of
text, more text on the slides

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:02.950
in order for these presentations
to stand for themselves.

00:07:02.950 --> 00:07:06.240
Next is the fact that your
reputation as a consulting firm

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:08.910
but also if you work
in-house as a project lead

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:09.940
for a big corporate,

00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:13.180
your reputation as the one
that creates these documents

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:14.240
is very much linked

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:16.250
to the quality of this document, right?

00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:20.660
So because these documents can
have a very high shelf life,

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:23.165
if there are any errors in
there, any inconsistencies,

00:07:23.165 --> 00:07:26.680
any imprecise statements, it will be known

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:30.940
that this was the document
created by BCG or by you.

00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:34.423
And years later, potentially
people still think about this

00:07:34.423 --> 00:07:36.500
look at this and we'll
have this in their mind.

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:38.750
Another important consideration
is that of course,

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:41.210
the composition of
teams changes over time.

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:43.960
So if a document is looked
at maybe a year from now,

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:46.873
in most cases, the consulting
firm the created that

00:07:46.873 --> 00:07:48.523
will not be at the company anymore.

00:07:48.523 --> 00:07:51.069
Or maybe also the employees
that work together

00:07:51.069 --> 00:07:53.036
with the consultants to
create this document,

00:07:53.036 --> 00:07:55.060
maybe they move to a new job,

00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:56.830
to a new employer, to a new company.

00:07:56.830 --> 00:07:59.497
So there might not even be
somebody who can be asked

00:07:59.497 --> 00:08:02.310
what was done at the time,
what really happened,

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:04.100
what numbers exactly to look at?

00:08:04.100 --> 00:08:06.010
And again, this is just one more reason

00:08:06.010 --> 00:08:08.449
why it's so important to
really create these documents

00:08:08.449 --> 00:08:11.450
at the high standards
that I try to teach you

00:08:11.450 --> 00:08:13.080
your honor videos on this channel,

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:14.230
and also in my course.

00:08:14.230 --> 00:08:16.140
Again, if you do want
to check the full course

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:17.250
that I have on this topic,

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:18.880
check out the link in
the video description.

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:21.510
So I do hope that this video
cleared up some confusion

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:24.082
on when exactly this style
of creating presentations

00:08:24.082 --> 00:08:27.032
is to be used, and when
also this is not necessary,

00:08:27.032 --> 00:08:29.605
not required to put that much effort

00:08:29.605 --> 00:08:31.380
into these presentations,

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:33.653
and also especially why
it is not to be confused

00:08:33.653 --> 00:08:37.040
with the Steve Jobs style, keynote style,

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:38.330
of creating presentations,

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:41.197
but why coinciding firms
usually create the presentations

00:08:41.197 --> 00:08:42.950
and a bit of a different style,

00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:44.960
and why this of course
is not only relevant

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:47.710
and important for consulting
firms, but for you as well,

00:08:47.710 --> 00:08:51.130
if you work in corporate and
banking one any other career

00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:52.000
where you are in a position

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:53.586
to frequently create presentations

00:08:53.586 --> 00:08:56.283
for C-level, senior
management type of audiences

00:08:56.283 --> 00:08:57.480
that you are talking to.

00:08:57.480 --> 00:08:59.170
So if you have any questions about that,

